      { "type": "text", "html": "<h2>Architectural notes</h2>" },
      { "type": "text", "html": "<h3>Boolean parameter optimization <i>(<code>int8_t</code> instead of <code>bool</code>)</i></h3>" },
      { "type": "text", "html": "To ensure strict binary compatibility (<b>ABI compliance</b>) between different compilers, languages (<b>C++, C#, Rust, Python</b>) and runtime environments, all logical parameters (<b>enable/disable flags</b>) are passed through the <code>int8_t</code> type instead of the standard <code>bool</code>:" },
      { "type": "text", "html": "<ul><li><b>The built-in <code>bool</code> problem:</b> In the <b>C++</b> standard, the size of the <code>bool</code> type is not strictly fixed and may differ depending on the platform and compiler settings, which is unacceptable when directly laying out <b>Shared Memory</b> at fixed offsets</li><li><b>Validation convention:</b> Any value <code>bValue != 0</code> is interpreted by the game handler as <b><code>true</code></b>, while <code>bValue == 0</code> is strictly equivalent to <b><code>false</code></b></li><li><b>How this is expressed in the documentation:</b> If a variable name has the <code>b-</code> suffix <i>(per Hungarian notation and Unreal Engine style)</i>, then its <code>int8_t</code> type must be interpreted as described above <b>^</b></li></ul>" },
      { "type": "text", "html": "<h3>Variable and register behavior on class change <i>(Command <code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_SET_CLASS</code>)</i></h3>" },
      { "type": "text", "html": "The element class change command <i>(<code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_SET_CLASS</code>)</i> <b>completely</b> recreates the element at the same GUID, preserving <b>only:</b> GUID, Transform and Visibility:" },
      { "type": "text", "html": "<ul><li><b>Full data reset:</b> Old values of all registers, State and Callback are fully wiped and reset to the default value for the given element. The new element starts with clean default data</li><li><b>Different interpretation:</b> Copying data between classes is meaningless, since each element interprets the same registers differently. That is exactly why their values are reset - to exclude logical errors</li><li><b>Preserving data via SDK:</b> If the old element data needs to be transferred to the new one, you must do it yourself in your program code:<ul><li>Get the current element data <i>(<code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_GET_STATE</code> / <code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_GET_CALLBACK</code> / <code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_GET_ALL_REGISTERS</code> / <code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_GET_STATE_AND_CALLBACK_AND_ALL_REGISTERS</code>)</i></li><li>Change the element class <i>(<code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_SET_CLASS</code>)</i></li><li>Write <i>(restore)</i> the element data <i>(<code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_SET_STATE</code> / <code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_SET_CALLBACK</code> / <code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_SET_ALL_REGISTERS</code> / <code>PSE_COMMAND_ELEMENT_SET_STATE_AND_CALLBACK_AND_ALL_REGISTERS</code>)</i></li></ul></li><li><b>But be careful:</b> Do not forget to check the register purpose table <i>(see <b>Concept > Elements</b>)</i> for the elements and verify exactly which data you are transferring, since for <b>two different elements the same register may radically differ in meaning and logic</b>!</li></ul>" }
    ]
  }
}
